Credential default mutation boundaries
======================================

Creating or promoting a default credential demotes live defaults only in the
same organization, exact site binding, scope and vendor. NULL site identifies
the organization-level group; it does not match every site. Previously the
demotion query ignored site_id, allowing a site-limited caller to unset defaults
at an inaccessible sibling site. Organization-level default writes could also
unset site-bound defaults. Explicit NULL vendors remain a distinct group.

Moving an existing default between sites, scopes or vendors now replaces the
destination group's default even if is_default is omitted. Explicit false and
non-default moves leave other defaults untouched. A metadata-only edit or an
unchanged group does not silently repair historical duplicate defaults.
Inactive live defaults retain the prior demotion behavior; deleted rows are
left untouched. No database migration or change to encrypted secrets is needed.

For an unscoped super administrator editing another organization's credential,
the credential owner determines both demotion scope and destination-site
validation. Previously those checks used the administrator's home organization,
which could alter the wrong organization's defaults or create an inconsistent
credential/site organization binding. Existing tenant filtering, permission
checks, site visibility and scoped API-key restrictions remain in place.
Organization-level credentials retain their established visibility policy.

Regression coverage uses real PostgreSQL rows and HTTP routes with synthetic
authenticated principals. Each request checks all existing credential records,
including encrypted secret columns; protected records retain their metadata.
Separate application processes exercise the same boundaries over loopback TCP.
The source export, dependency audits and shipping qualification are separately
bound to their recorded results. No real login or vendor operation is implied.

Concurrent management API writes now acquire a PostgreSQL transaction advisory
lock scoped to the credential owner organization before changing records. The
lock covers empty groups, including two concurrent creates, and releases at
commit/rollback. Other organizations remain independent. Updates and deletes
reload the credential after waiting and recheck its current site binding before
mutation; a moved-out-of-scope or deleted credential returns a fixed 404.
Creation without an owner organization returns a fixed 403 before database writes.

Fourteen additional PostgreSQL cases use independent committed transactions:
eight concurrent create/update promotions across site/global and NULL/named
vendor groups, four stale-binding move/delete cases, and one cross-organization
availability control, and one refusal for creation without an owner organization.
Thirteen reproduce incorrect state, unauthorized late mutation or a server error
on the previous implementation; the control succeeds on both. These
cases use synthetic ASGI identities, not real login or device traffic.

This API lock does not impose database uniqueness on independent restore jobs,
raw SQL or other writers that do not take the same lock. It does not automatically
repair historical duplicates or inconsistent bindings, audit all credential
input validation, or prove concurrent permission/session revocation safety.
No automatic default-selection consumer was found during this focused review;
the reproduced impact is incorrect stored credential state, not a demonstrated
device authentication outage. Enterprise acceptance remains a separate gate.
